PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C

The question requires identifying the *least* plausible of the eight explanations for the consistent blanks. After independent evaluation of historical context (Bansenshukai compiled 1676 by Fujibayashi, all surviving copies post-date the lost original, Oniwaban founded decades later, Lady Saigō’s documented biography, and known ninja practices such as aburidashi), C stands out as the weakest.

C rests on three unsupported claims: (1) Lady Saigō was Hattori Hanzo’s stepdaughter (no primary source supports this), (2) she personally employed “kunoichi techniques” described in a later Iga manual, and (3) the Tokugawa regime therefore ordered the redaction of an entire section across every copy to protect regime legitimacy. These assertions are speculative conspiracy layered onto chronologically and biographically mismatched figures. The other options, while varying in likelihood, at least align with attested period practices (moral censorship by copyists, physical wear, mnemonic or cryptographic conventions, or later bureaucratic control). C therefore requires the largest number of unverified historical leaps and is the least plausible.}

